---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import SiteCheckForm from "../../components/SiteCheckForm.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Watches chosen sites for lookalike content", them: "Yes", us: "-" },
  { label: "Stops scrapers and feed copying", them: "No", us: "-" },
  { label: "Protects your text once it leaves your store", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Proves a stolen description was yours", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Produces evidence for a DMCA takedown", them: "Match reports, not ownership proof", us: "Yes" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Copy monitors vs Kirpik"
  description="A plain comparison of copy monitors and plagiarism-style scanners with Kirpik: what a match report shows, whether monitoring prevents content theft, and why similarity is not authorship."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Copy monitors vs Kirpik"
    lead="A copy monitor scans other sites for content that matches yours and reports what it finds. A match report shows similarity between two pages; it does not show whose text it was first."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What a copy monitor does</h2>
      <p>
        A monitor works server-side, comparing pages from sites it scans against yours, the same family
        of techniques as plagiarism scanners. It can catch partial matches and light rewording, and it
        does the watching for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three apps in the Shopify anti-theft category describe this approach in their listings, as
        recorded in June and July 2026. Spy Blocker scans domains you choose for "copied images, copied
        text, and similar product pages" and assembles evidence packs. IP Moat runs marketplace scanning
        with a managed takedown service. Mintall Keep lists IP monitoring alongside its provenance
        records. Each is compared in full on its own page:
        <a href="/compare/copycat-alert-product-monitor/">Spy Blocker</a>,
        <a href="/compare/ip-moat/">IP Moat</a>,
        <a href="/compare/mintallkeep/">Mintall Keep</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Does it prevent content theft?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A monitor acts after the event: by the time a match is reported, the
        content has already been taken. Monitoring changes nothing on your store, so Shopify still
        serves your content through the machine-readable feeds (JSON, XML and ATOM) that scrapers
        actually read, and a monitor sees only the sites it scans; stolen text pasted anywhere else
        stays unseen until someone finds it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik does not claim to prevent content theft: it <strong>protects</strong> content. The
        watermark travels inside the text itself, so stolen content stays identifiable and provable
        wherever it surfaces. Kirpik adds no scripts to your storefront, so it runs alongside any
        blocker without conflict.
      </p>

      <h2>Can it prove infringement?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A match report shows similarity, not authorship: two pages carry
        substantially the same words, but nothing in the report shows who wrote them first. Where a
        listing describes evidence packs, that is per its listing; the packs are built from matches, and
        a match shows similarity rather than authorship.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik proves infringement through the watermark and the Proof of Ownership Certificate.
        Detection reads your watermark out of the stolen text, and the certificate records dated
        fingerprints, the infringing page's timestamps, Wayback Machine corroboration and a public
        verification page, ready for a DMCA takedown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="tablehead">Copy monitors vs Kirpik</h2>
      <CompareTable themLabel="Copy monitors" rows={rows} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteCheckForm tone="band" heading="Is your store's content protected?" cta="Check my store">
    <Fragment slot="sub">
      Do you use <strong>a copy monitor</strong> or any other "Content Protection" App? Enter your
      Shopify store address to see if your content is actually protected.
    </Fragment>
  </SiteCheckForm>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <section class="fineprint">
        <p>
          This page describes scanning services as presented in their own public Shopify App Store
          listings, June and July 2026; capabilities vary by service and listings change. The
          limitations discussed are general properties of matching techniques rather than defects of
          any app. App names and trademarks belong to their respective owners and are used for
          identification only. Nothing here is legal advice.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablehead { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 1.15rem + 1.2vw, 2rem); }
  .fineprint { padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .fineprint p { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 78ch; }
  .fineprint a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
